            <ArticleTitle>Primary non-Hodgkin's lymphoma presenting as radicular syndrome: report of two cases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>113-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00296-009-0898-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report two cases of primary Non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma in the spine leading to radicular compression secondary to infiltration of lumbar body vertebras. The two patients were free of either nodular or other extra-nodular disease. Treatment consisted of chemotherapy alone, one patient have had a cauda equina syndrome and surgical decompression was performed in emergency. The patients were in remission for 20 months after diagnosis. A review is given for the incidence of primary vertebral localization of lymphoma, its diagnosis, treatment and prognosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
